\subsection{Testbench and Simulation}
A common testbench instantiates both designs, drives them with the same clock and \texttt{start} pulse, and applies the input values
\begin{equation}
a=2,\quad b=3,\quad c=4,\quad d=5,\quad e=1.
\end{equation}
The expected result is
\begin{equation}
z = (2+3)(4+5)+1 = 5\cdot 9 + 1 = 46.
\end{equation}
The QuestaSim waveform in Fig.~\ref{fig:wave} shows that both designs produce $z=46$. The signal \texttt{done\_2alu} becomes high one clock cycle earlier than \texttt{done\_1alu}, confirming in simulation that the two-ALU implementation finishes earlier, exactly as predicted by the cycle counts of the C++ scheduler.

